The array has been created under the variable List (bad naming I know).
We know want to know how many items are in the array.
we do this by:
count=${#list[@]}
When the # key is placed after the { it tells it to count whatever else is in the variable (or in the } in this instance).
When we place the @ symbole within the [] it is telling it to select all the items within the array. 
So in theory we are telling the command line to count ALL the items within the array and place them within the variable count.

With this we can create our for loop.

    #!/bin/bash
    
    List=($(ls -a))
    count=${#List[@]}
    
    for (( i = 0 ; i < count ; i++))
    do
    
            if [ List[$i] == *.txt ]
            then
                    echo "Txt file : List[$i]"
            else
                    echo "No txt file: List[$i]"
            fi
    done

In theory this for loop should work, it goes through the amount of items in the array shown by i < count. 
For every item it meets it checks if there is a .txt at the end (we use the * sign to signal we only care whats at the end of the file)
